docs/architecture/commands.md:3:Commands are the undo/redo and persistence boundary for mutations. The UI does not directly mutate the domain model.
docs/architecture/overview.md:15:## Boundary rules
docs/architecture/overview.md:20:- Selectors are the boundary between domain state and rendering adapters.
docs/contributing/development-setup.md:31:npm run test:architecture
docs/contributing/development-setup.md:44:architecture-audit/    # reviewed refactor-wave artifacts
docs/contributing/testing.md:11:npm run test:architecture
docs/contributing/testing.md:12:npm run test:architecture --workspace=frontend
docs/contributing/testing.md:33:- `frontend/tests/unit/architecture` — architecture/static boundary tests.
docs/contributing/testing.md:44:Run focused tests for touched modules and the full cadence for implementation waves. Store long validation logs under the relevant `architecture-audit/cycle-*/validation/` directory.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:4:**Basis:** signed-off Phase 1 baseline only (`architecture-audit/baseline/decision.md`).  
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:10:This plan orders waves by architectural centrality, not ease of implementation. The first waves establish the domain/application boundary and authoritative contracts because later persistence, rendering, tooling, UI composition, backend alignment, and documentation waves depend on those decisions. Each wave is intentionally scoped to fit one Phase 3 cycle: scope check, two proposals, dual-reviewer convergence, implementation, validation, preview refresh, and implementation log.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:12:The signed-off baseline ranks the domain/application dependency inversion around `World` as the highest maintainability risk because `World` imports services and command/application classes while commands also import `World` (`architecture-audit/baseline/risk-register.md`, R1; `architecture-audit/baseline/architecture-map.md`, “Cross-layer leaks and circular/dependency inversions”). The same baseline identifies god-modules, duplicated state paths, persistence/private-cast coupling, UI-to-operation imports, broad `any` contracts, dead legacy workspace files, rendering concentration, disconnected backend validation, and weak architecture tests as the next central risks (`architecture-audit/baseline/risk-register.md`, R2–R13; `architecture-audit/baseline/quality-report.md`, Q1–Q22).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:21:4. Do not pull peripheral UI/docs cleanup ahead of unresolved domain, contract, persistence, command/undo, rendering, or tool-state boundary work.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:22:5. Preserve aggressive deletion: obsolete shims, dead files, placeholder docs, compatibility branches, and duplicate paths must be removed rather than wrapped.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:26:Each implementation wave must run the mandatory validation cadence recorded under that wave’s `validation/` directory: root/workspace lint where defined, focused Vitest for touched modules, full Vitest, root/workspace build where defined, Playwright/e2e for frontend/user-facing/persistence/API changes, and live preview refresh/reachability probe. Waves that touch only documentation in later phases may justify skipping e2e only in their implementation log.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:30:## Wave 001 — Invert the World/application boundary
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:43:- focused domain/application boundary tests
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:47:The baseline states that `World` is the canonical root but imports `DependencyService`, `Command`, initialization factories, process-tree operations, and `CommandStack` while commands import `World`, creating the most central inversion (`risk-register.md` R1; `domain-model.md` “Coupling observations”; `quality-report.md` Q3). It also identifies `Element2D.realize2D(world: World)` and `styleService` walking `World`/`ProcessTree` as domain-internal root coupling (`domain-model.md` corrections; `risk-register.md` R11). Baseline corrections also identify descriptor registration as a runtime composition point: `DescriptorRegistry.ts` maps element classes to descriptor logic and `registerDescriptors.ts` is invoked before app mount, so Wave 001 proposals must evaluate this coupling when removing application/service imports from the domain boundary (`inventory.md` corrections; `architecture-map.md` addendum).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:51:`World` becomes a domain-owned model root with no imports from commands, command stacks, application services, Vue, or persistence. Initialization orchestration lives outside the domain root in an application/use-case layer. Domain realization, descriptor registration, and visibility dependencies are expressed through narrow domain ports/contexts rather than the whole `World` object where practical within this wave. If `styleService.ts` still needs process-tree-specific cleanup after the `World` dependency is removed, that residual ProcessTree ownership question is explicitly deferred to Wave 005 rather than hidden in this boundary wave.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:58:- Delete tests that assert initialization must be invoked through `World` if they exist; replace with tests for the new application initializer.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:65:- Architecture test coverage is added or updated to prevent domain-to-command/service imports for the boundary established here.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:86:The baseline identifies `ProjectionPlane.ts` as a 1,797-line domain god-module combining realization classes, base abstraction, element maps/caches, dependency side effects, construction helpers, fixed planes, and auxiliary planes (`quality-report.md` Q2; `architecture-map.md` “God-modules”; `risk-register.md` R2). It also flags `realize2D(world: any)` / `realize3D(world: any)` as weak central contracts (`quality-report.md` Q8; `risk-register.md` R6).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:170:Command/undo exposes explicit typed APIs for inspection, restoration, and serialization. Persistence uses one chosen contract — typed operation descriptors/registry or a deliberate domain snapshot — without private-field casts, migration branches, corrupted-operation compatibility fallbacks, or broad `any` shapes at the main boundary.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:177:- Delete obsolete serialization coverage tests that encode the old switch/private-cast architecture; replace with contract tests.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:182:- Serialization contracts are discriminated and typed at the boundary chosen by the wave.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:188:Depends on Waves 001–003 because persistence must target the cleaned authoritative domain model and not preserve duplicate store/dead workspace paths.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:200:- `frontend/src/components/panels/ActionsPanel.vue` only as needed to consume the cleaned boundary
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:205:The baseline states that process history and dependency tracking overlap: `ProcessTree` still exposes deprecated `addAction()` and deprecated dependency queries while `DependencyService` is the active dependency boundary (`domain-model.md`; `quality-report.md` Q14; `risk-register.md` R7). `ActionsPanel.vue` also builds process-tree operations directly from UI (`architecture-map.md`; `risk-register.md` R5).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:209:ProcessTree owns construction history only. Dependency queries live in the single dependency boundary established by earlier waves. UI panels consume application use-cases or typed process-tree commands rather than deprecated direct APIs.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:236:- `frontend/src/composables/useCommandExecution.ts` if it remains unused or must become a thin adapter around the new command-execution boundary
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:243:This wave is the immediate replacement for the original Wave 006 implementation slice required by `architecture-audit/cycle-006-ui-command-controllers/scope-check.md`. It is limited to the active canvas/tool surface. `ActionsPanel.vue` and `Toolbar.vue` are explicitly excluded except as named residual command leaks scheduled for Wave 006b. If implementation discovers a necessary change to those panel/toolbar surfaces, stop and run a reviewed plan-edit mini-cycle before broadening this wave.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:247:The baseline flags UI components directly importing `Command` and concrete operations (`architecture-map.md` leak 3; `risk-register.md` R5; `quality-report.md` Q1, Q4, Q19). The Wave 006 scope check confirmed that the active canvas/tool path still constructs and executes commands directly: `SvgCanvas.vue` imports command/operation classes and pushes commands for point drag and style edits, tool classes receive `world` plus `commandStack`, `ArgumentCollectorTool` owns an open-command lifecycle, and concrete tools import factories and process-tree operations. The same scope check found that `ActionsPanel.vue` and `Toolbar.vue` are real but separate command-construction surfaces with distinct controller concepts and validation surfaces, requiring the 006a/006b split before implementation.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:259:- Delete `frontend/src/composables/useCommandExecution.ts` if static search confirms it is unused by active source; otherwise delete its broad legacy executor shape and replace it with a thin typed adapter around the new boundary.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:291:This wave begins only after Wave 006a completes and its implementation log records any remaining panel/toolbar command leaks. It must not reopen the canvas/tool controller extraction except for consuming the stable application command/use-case boundary created in 006a. If the toolbar example construction is judged debug-only, prefer deletion over migrating a dead shortcut.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:295:The baseline flags direct UI-to-command/operation imports as an architectural leak (`architecture-map.md` leak 3; `risk-register.md` R5; `quality-report.md` Q1, Q4, Q19). The Wave 006 scope check found that `ActionsPanel.vue` directly imports `Command` plus concrete process-tree operations and constructs/pushes commands in panel handlers, while `Toolbar.vue` directly imports `Command`, `CommandStack`, `Vector3`, and concrete world operations for the Alt+E example setup. The scope check also concluded these panel/toolbar paths are separate from canvas tools because they require process-history edit use-cases, debug/example construction decisions, and independent validation.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:306:- Delete the Toolbar Alt+E example/debug shortcut entirely if review/implementation determines it is not user-facing architecture worth preserving.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:308:- Delete any duplicate panel/toolbar factory layers that duplicate the 006a command-construction/use-case boundary.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:315:- No new command-construction boundary parallel to 006a is introduced; panel/toolbar code consumes the stable application/use-case boundary or a clearly separate process-history use-case boundary.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:320:Depends on Wave 006a so panel/toolbar cleanup can consume the stable command-execution/use-case boundary established for canvas/tools. Wave 007 remains after 006a and 006b so rendering selectors are not extracted while unresolved central UI command-controller leaks remain.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:338:The baseline identifies `PaperService` as a 993-line hotspot importing domain objects, world types, presentation primitives, style resolution, and geometry helpers, while `RenderService` contains test-only paths and production uses `SVGRenderer` (`architecture-map.md`; `quality-report.md` Q13, Q15; `risk-register.md` R8). `SvgCanvas.vue` is the primary 2D rendering/controller god component (`quality-report.md` Q1).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:359:Depends on Waves 001–006 because selectors must target the cleaned domain/use-case boundaries and should not preserve UI operation construction.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:375:The baseline identifies `ThreeView.vue` as a 1,201-line god component combining scene setup, projection-plane rendering, 2D-on-3D rendering, 3D elements, guide geometry, hinge lines, animation, resize, cleanup, domain realization access, and store access (`quality-report.md` Q5; `architecture-map.md`; `risk-register.md` R8).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:413:The baseline classifies `App.vue` as a god shell that creates `World`, `CommandStack`, Drive service, local project state, autosave timers, keyboard/delete/select-all behavior, active-plane state, project lifecycle, and command coordination (`quality-report.md` Q4; `architecture-map.md` “Entry points and workspace boundaries”; `risk-register.md` R2, R5).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:437:## Wave 010 — Resolve backend/frontend project-contract architecture
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:451:The baseline finds the backend is a parallel/disconnected Express/file-storage path with broad `any` DTOs, root scripts skip backend validation, and the frontend imports `ProjectMetadata` directly from backend source while using frontend `ProjectService`/Drive rather than backend routes (`architecture-map.md` backend sections and corrections; `quality-report.md` Q9, Q18, Q20, Q22; `risk-register.md` R9, R12).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:455:The project has one explicit backend stance: either delete the unused backend/project API from the active architecture, or make it a real validated boundary with a shared typed contract and root validation. Do not preserve a disconnected backend plus frontend source import leak.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:468:- Frontend persistence architecture is consistent with the decision made in Wave 004.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:472:Depends on Wave 004 because backend project DTOs must align with the chosen frontend persistence contract, and on Wave 009 if app shell persistence routing is moved first.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:476:## Wave 011 — Tighten architecture tests and root validation gates
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:480:- `frontend/tests/unit/architecture/**`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:484:- test helpers for import-boundary assertions
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:489:The baseline says existing architecture tests cover narrow rules (no command subclasses and serialization coverage) while allowing broader violations: domain-to-command/service imports, UI-to-operation imports, and duplicate store/domain ownership (`quality-report.md` Q19; `risk-register.md` R10). It also reports root scripts skip backend despite workspaces declaring frontend and backend (`quality-report.md` Q18; `risk-register.md` R9).
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:497:- Delete architecture tests that encode obsolete boundaries from pre-refactor structure.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:503:- Architecture tests fail on domain-to-command/service imports, UI-to-operation imports, duplicate geometry state ownership, and forbidden frontend→backend implementation imports according to the boundaries established by earlier waves.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:509:Depends on Waves 001–010 so tests freeze the cleaned target architecture, not the current violated one.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:551:## Wave 013 — Reconcile docs/specs with the cleaned architecture
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:555:- `docs/architecture/**`
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:564:The baseline inventories the current `specs/001-dihedral-learning-mvp/` tree and the docs areas under `docs/architecture/`, `docs/api/`, `docs/guide/`, and `docs/contributing/testing.md` (`inventory.md` Specs and Docs sections). It also notes that current code comments still cite the original MVP spec for world/process/command concepts and that README-only operation placeholders are deletion candidates (`inventory.md` corrections; `quality-report.md` Q21; `risk-register.md` R13). Earlier objectives require docs/comments waves after central domain, persistence, rendering, tool-state, and UI composition work.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:568:Documentation describes the architecture that now exists after the refactor waves. Obsolete specs, architecture pages, comments, and examples that preserve old mental models are deleted or rewritten.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:573:- Delete source comments that cite obsolete architecture/spec claims.
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:584:Depends on all earlier structural waves. Docs/comments intentionally come last so they do not freeze an intermediate architecture.
